## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to implement a hardware-style popcount without loops or built-in functions, demonstrating understanding of bitwise manipulation techniques.

## Key Concepts
- Parallel bit counting using bitmasks
- 32-bit unsigned arithmetic with logical shifts
- Elimination of branches and loops for performance

## Code Example
```javascript
function countBits32(n) {
  n = n - ((n >>> 1) & 0x55555555);
  n = (n & 0x33333333) + ((n >>> 2) & 0x33333333);
  n = (n + (n >>> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  n = n + (n >>> 8);
  n = n + (n >>> 16);
  return n & 0x3F;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would this differ for a 64-bit implementation?
- Can you optimize this for specific input patterns?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests advanced 128-bit bit-twiddling, boundary shifts, and correctness under rotation.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit values represented as hi/lo 64-bit halves
- Rotation across a 64-bit boundary
- Handling n in [0,127] with minimal branching in production

## Code Example
```javascript
uint128 rotateLeft128(uint128 x, unsigned n){
  n &= 127; // modulo 128
  if (n < 64){
    uint64_t hi = (x.hi << n) | (x.lo >> (64 - n));
    uint64_t lo = (x.lo << n) | (x.hi >> (64 - n));
    return (uint128){.hi = hi, .lo = lo};
  } else {
    unsigned k = n - 64;
    uint64_t hi = (x.lo << k) | (x.hi >> (64 - k));
    uint64_t lo = (x.hi << k) | (x.lo >> (64 - k));
    return (uint128){.hi = hi, .lo = lo};
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- Can you make it fully branchless with masks?
- How would you adapt for different word sizes or endianness?

## Why This Is Asked

- Demonstrates ability to replace branches with bit-twiddling for performance.
- Applies a well-known, portable technique to a common memory-allocation problem.
- Handles edge cases and overflow without loops.

## Key Concepts
- Prefix-fill trick to propagate the highest set bit.
- Branchless arithmetic to compute next power of two.
- Overflow behavior on 32-bit boundaries.

## Code Example

```c
uint32_t nextPowerOfTwo32(uint32_t n) {
  // Assumes n > 0. No explicit branches.
  n--;
  n |= n >> 1;
  n |= n >> 2;
  n |= n >> 4;
  n |= n >> 8;
  n |= n >> 16;
  return n + 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How does this behave for n = 0x80000000 or 0xFFFFFFFF?
- Can you adapt this to 64-bit inputs with no loops?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests applying known bit-hacks to multi-lane data without branching, simulating performance-critical kernels.

## Key Concepts
- Parallel bit counting across lanes
- 128-bit to four 32-bit lanes mapping
- Branchless arithmetic
- Endianness and lane indexing

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

static inline uint32_t popcount32(uint32_t x) {
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x55555555);
  x = (x & 0x33333333) + ((x >> 2) & 0x33333333);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  x = x + (x >> 8);
  x = x + (x >> 16);
  return x & 0x3F;
}

uint32_t popcountPerLane128(uint128 x) {
  uint32_t lo0 = (uint32_t)(x.lo);
  uint32_t lo1 = (uint32_t)(x.lo >> 32);
  uint32_t hi0 = (uint32_t)(x.hi);
  uint32_t hi1 = (uint32_t)(x.hi >> 32);

  uint32_t p0 = popcount32(lo0);
  uint32_t p1 = popcount32(lo1);
  uint32_t p2 = popcount32(hi0);
  uint32_t p3 = popcount32(hi1);

  return (p3 << 24) | (p2 << 16) | (p1 << 8) | p0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to 64-bit lanes?
- What are the performance implications on modern CPUs with SIMD intrinsics?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of 64-bit bit reversals and cross-half manipulation without loops. It ensures ability to compose operations on 128-bit types.

## Key Concepts
- 64-bit bit reversal with masks
- 128-bit struct handling
- Non-branching composition

## Code Example
```javascript
// Outline only: reverse64 implemented with a sequence of mask/shifts
static inline uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t x) {
  x = (x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) << 1 | (x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) << 2 | (x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL;
  x = (x & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL) << 4 | (x >> 4) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL;
  x = ((x & 0x00ff00ff00ff00ffULL) << 8) | ((x >> 8) & 0x00ff00ff00ff00ffULL);
  x = (x << 48) | ((x & 0xffff) << 16) | ((x >> 16) & 0xffff);
  return x;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- Extend to 128-bit popcount-free reversal across 16-byte boundary
- Compare against a byte-by-byte reversal approach for throughput

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of bit-level data packing and word-level efficiency.

## Key Concepts
- Bit spreading (dilating)
- Inline masks and shifts for 32-bit results
- Morton codes for spatial indexing

## Code Example
```javascript
uint32_t part1by1(uint16_t n) {
  uint32_t x = n;
  x &= 0x0000FFFF;
  x = (x | (x << 8)) & 0x00FF00FF;
  x = (x | (x << 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  x = (x | (x << 2)) & 0x33333333;
  x = (x | (x << 1)) & 0x55555555;
  return x;
}
uint32_t morton2D(uint16_t x, uint16_t y) {
  return (part1by1(y) << 1) | part1by1(x);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to 3D Morton codes (x,y,z) and 64-bit result? 
- How would you adapt for non-power-of-two bit widths or streaming data?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to perform full-width bit reversal in constant time using SWAR patterns, not per-bit loops.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit composition as hi/lo 64-bit
- SWAR bit-reversal: 1,2,4,8,16,32-bit block swaps
- Final cross-half swap to complete reversal

## Code Example
```javascript
// 64-bit reversal helper
static uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t x){
  x = (x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL | (x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) << 1;
  x = (x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL | (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) << 2;
  x = (x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL | (x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) << 4;
  x = (x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL | (x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8;
  x = (x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL | (x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16;
  x = (x >> 32) & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL | (x & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL) << 32;
  return x;
}
uint128 reverse128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r;
  r.lo = reverse64(x.hi);
  r.hi = reverse64(x.lo);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to larger widths (256-bit) with minimal extra code?
- What intrinsics or compiler options help optimize this on typical CPUs?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to derive complex bit-pattern metrics with constant-time bit tricks, not brute force.

## Key Concepts

- Identify run starts with n & ~(n << 1)
- Implement a portable popcount64 using parallel masks
- Edge-case handling for 0 and large runs

## Code Example

```c
static inline unsigned popcount64(uint64_t x) {
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}

static inline unsigned countRuns64(uint64_t n) {
  return popcount64(n & ~(n << 1));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt to an 128-bit value?
- What are the performance implications on large-scale bitmap indexes in practice?

## Why This Is Asked
Demonstrates practical bitfield packing/unpacking and rotation without branches, mirroring real-world protocol parsing.

## Key Concepts
- Field extraction with masks and shifts
- Recombination to rotate fields in place
- Boundary safety for 10-bit widths

## Code Example
```javascript
function rotateFieldsLeft32(x) {
  const A = x & 0x3FF;
  const B = (x >>> 10) & 0x3FF;
  const C = (x >>> 20) & 0x3FF;
  return (B) | (C << 10) | (A << 20);
}
```

