## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about data-parallel bit tricks and boundary-safe shifts without branches.

## Key Concepts

- Byte-wise (SWAR) operations across 128-bit lanes
- Constructing per-byte masks to constrain operations to byte boundaries
- Handling n in 0..7 without loops or conditionals

## Code Example

```cpp
// outline of approach (conceptual; leverages 128-bit value split into bytes)
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
static inline uint128 perByteRotateLeft128(uint128 x, unsigned n){
    uint128 M = { 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFULL, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFULL };
    uint128 left  = { .hi = (x.hi << n) | (x.lo >> (8 - n)), .lo = x.lo << n };
    uint128 right = { .hi = (x.hi >> (8 - n)), .lo = (x.lo >> (8 - n)) };
    return { .hi = (left.hi & M.hi) | (right.hi & ~M.hi),
             .lo = (left.lo & M.lo) | (right.lo & ~M.lo) };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you generalize to 32-bit lanes or to 256-bit types?
- What are the performance implications on modern compilers and ISAs?

## Why This Is Asked
Fresh bit-hack that demonstrates mastery of fixed-step SWAR technique and portability across languages.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR fixed-masks and staged swaps
- Bitwise rotation-free reversal
- Unsigned 32-bit wrap handling in JavaScript/TypeScript

## Code Example
```javascript
function reverseBits32(n) {
  n = ((n >>> 1) & 0x55555555) | ((n & 0x55555555) << 1);
  n = ((n >>> 2) & 0x33333333) | ((n & 0x33333333) << 2);
  n = ((n >>> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F) | ((n & 0x0F0F0F0F) << 4);
  n = ((n >>> 8) & 0x00FF00FF) | ((n & 0x00FF00FF) << 8);
  n = (n >>> 16) | (n << 16);
  return n >>> 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for 64-bit integers in languages with 64-bit ops?
- How to verify correctness with quick property tests (e.g., n reversing twice yields n)?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests cross-word bit ops and branchless parity reduction. It exercises combining two 64-bit halves and a compact reduction, scalable to wider architectures.

## Key Concepts

- Parity via XOR folding
- 128-bit composition hi^lo
- Branchless reductions

## Code Example

```c
int parity128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo) {
  uint64_t x = hi ^ lo;
  x ^= x >> 32;
  x ^= x >> 16;
  x ^= x >> 8;
  x ^= x >> 4;
  return x & 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to a 256-bit value?  - Can you adapt this for streaming parity on large data chunks?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to implement a branchless popcount for 128-bit data using only bitwise ops, a core skill in performance-critical code paths like fingerprinting or anomaly detection.

## Key Concepts

- Branchless bit counting (SWAR)
- Apply per-64-bit halves independently
- Combine results without overflow or branches

## Code Example

```javascript
// Conceptual: 64-bit helper (C-like pseudocode)
unsigned int popcount64(unsigned long long x){
  x -= (x>>1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x>>2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x>>4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (unsigned int)((x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56);
}
unsigned int popcount128(uint128 x){
  return popcount64(x.hi) + popcount64(x.lo);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt if you needed to accumulate counts in a streaming fashion?
- How does this interact with endianness in cross-language bindings?

## Why This Is Asked
3D Morton codes enable fast spatial indexing in grid-like domains; a branchless, fixed-bit spreading approach scales predictably. 

## Key Concepts
- Morton codes for 3D require interleaving bits from three coordinates.
- Bit spreading (dilating) inserts two zero bits between each original bit; a fixed sequence of masks/shifts yields a branchless implementation.
- BigInt vs. Number in JavaScript: use BigInt for 64-bit results if implementing in JS; otherwise 64-bit types in C/C++ handle it natively.

## Code Example
```javascript
function part1by2(n) {
  let x = BigInt(n) & 0x1fffffn; // 21 bits
  x = (x | (x << 32n)) & 0x1f00000000ffffn;
  x = (x | (x << 16n)) & 0x1f0000ff0000ffn;
  x = (x | (x << 8n)) & 0x100f00f00f00f00fn;
  x = (x | (x << 4n)) & 0x10c30c30c30c30c3n;
  x = (x | (x << 2n)) & 0x1249249249249249n;
  return x;
}
function morton3D(x, y, z) {
  return part1by2(x) | (part1by2(y) << 1n) | (part1by2(z) << 2n);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you verify correctness for random inputs? 
- How to adapt to larger coordinates or alternative word sizes?
